  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWestern Kansas remains well within the warm/moist conveyor belt\nof the mid-latitude cyclone centered over Colorado. The upper\nlow will lift north-northeast across western Nebraska on \nMonday. A storm track of this type will lead to negligible cold\nfront push and will help keep the remnant warm/moist conveyor\nbelt across southern Kansas. As tonight and early Monday\nmorning's remnant showers move off to the northeast, the cold\nfront will push fairly slowly south across western Kansas with\nsoutherly to southwesterly (yet still fairly moist) ahead of the\nfront. Given the continued fairly high precipitable water values\nof 1.00 to 1.20 inches Monday late aftenoon and evening and\nsurface-based CAPE values 500 to 1000 J/kg thanks to a little\nbit of insolation, additional showers and thunderstorms will\nlikely develop generally south and east of a Liberal to Dodge\nCity to Larned line. NBM POPs of 40-70% appear good with no \nadjustment necessary. One or two of the storms late \nafternoon/early evening may produce some small hail and/or gusty\nwinds, but given the absence of large scale CAPE greater than \n1000 J/kg, no severe hail or wind gusts are expected. \n\nMoving ahead through the week, the theme will be warm\ntemperatures as the overall large scale pattern will revert\nquickly back to lower heights in the west with higher mid level\nheights over the southeastern CONUS. Thus, persistent southwest\nflow throughout much of the troposphere will favor warm\ntemperatures. Wednesday and Thursday will be the warmest days\nwith highs both days well into the mid to perhaps upper 70s.\nNighttime lows will be nowhere near freezing temperatures\nthrough at least next Sunday with lows most mornings in the\nupper 40s west to mid to upper 50s across south central Kansas.",
